  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is corrosion? Explain the electrochemical theory of rusting of iron and write the reactions involved in the rusting of iron. (3 marks)', 'Corrosion is the slow deterioration of a metal by chemical or electrochemical reaction with its surroundings - air, moisture, acids. Rusting of iron is electrochemical: a film of water containing dissolved O₂ and CO₂ (carbonic acid) acts as an electrolyte, and different spots on the iron become anodes and cathodes, forming tiny galvanic cells.
Anode (oxidation): Fe(s) → Fe²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻.
Cathode (reduction): O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O(l).
Overall: 2Fe + O₂ + 4H⁺ → 2Fe²⁺ + 2H₂O. The Fe²⁺ ions are further oxidised by air to Fe³⁺, which forms hydrated iron(III) oxide: 4Fe²⁺ + O₂ + (4 + 2x)H₂O → 2Fe₂O₃·xH₂O + 8H⁺. Fe₂O₃·xH₂O is rust.', null, '[{"point": "Corrosion: slow destruction of a metal by chemical/ electrochemical reaction with its environment (air, moisture, acids)", "marks": 0.5}, {"point": "Electrochemical theory: in a water film with dissolved O₂/ CO₂, part of the iron surface is the anode and another part the cathode (mini galvanic cell)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Anode: Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻; cathode: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O (or O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻); Fe²⁺ oxidised to Fe³⁺ → 2Fe²⁺ + ½O₂ + (2 + x)H₂O → Fe₂O₃·xH₂O + 4H⁺ (rust)", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
